 :adr/title "CLAUDE.md holds invariants; skills hold procedure — splitting the always-loaded agent instructions"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-08"
 :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"]
 :adr/repos ["com-junkawasaki/root"]
 :adr/related ["adr-2609062600-agent-instructions-generated-from-one-source"]
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nCLAUDE.md had grown to 154,842 characters (256,849 bytes) and crossed the harness limit of 150,000, which prints a warning at every session start. The limit is the symptom, not the problem. The file is loaded in full into every session, every subagent, and every scheduled bot on this workspace, so its size is paid on every turn — and the parts being paid for were mostly not invariants.\n\nMeasured before the split, six sections held 46% of the file:\n\n- CI/CD is murakumo fleet — 28,283 bytes\n- Git operations — 23,473\n- Use the `compile` route for `.kotoba` — 19,900\n- Two kinds of cannot-write in `.kotoba` — 18,897\n- `.cljc` / `.kotoba` runtime priority — 14,981\n- UI/UX standard (jp-go-dds) — 13,880\n\nEach of those is a small number of standing rules wrapped in a large amount of recorded incident: dated measurements, the shape of a bug someone hit once, corrections of earlier corrections. The document says of itself, in the fleet-ci section, that what belongs there is *how to measure*, not *what was measured* — and then carries several thousand characters of measured values, having twice already noticed and recorded that it did so.\n\nThe cost of that is not disk. A rule nobody finishes reading is the same as a rule that is not there.\n\n## Decision\n\n**CLAUDE.md holds only what must hold without reading anything else. Procedure, evidence, and traps move into skills.**\n\nThis is not a new mechanism. The document already delegated this way for `west-pin-advance`, `git-cleanup-conflict`, `kotoba-uiux`, `large-binary-datalad`, `secrets-location-map`, `build-actor`, and `new-project-scaffold`, and the west-pin section states the pattern explicitly: delegate the operational surface, restate inline only the rules that still bite when the skill is not read.\n\nThree new skills were created, and one existing skill was extended. Every moved block was moved **verbatim** — extracted with `sed` from the pre-split file and appended under a header naming this ADR — so the split cannot lose a rule by paraphrase:\n\n- `fleet-ci-gates` — gate authoring, `:include-ext`, node egress, signing keys, placement authority, LPT job assignment, the three checks before fixing a red gate, how to ask GitHub whether Actions are enabled and how to measure their billing.\n- `git-operations` — why shallow was retired and how to detect it, when ancestry answers lie, `(forced update)` versus a real force-push, the west.yml single-entry route, sync order, deploy inclusion, worktrees not isolating the object store.\n- `kotoba-authoring` — runtime priority evidence, the amu compile route and its measured blockers, permanent-versus-temporary classification and where to read it, native AOT status, the design-system migration order, JVM-free acceptance.\n- `kotoba-uiux` (extended) — the UI/UX standard and the design-quality-score sections in full.\n\n## What deliberately stayed inline\n\n- **The eight questions before writing a check or believing a green one.** It is repo-wide and applies to every task, not to fleet gates; it was promoted from H3 to H2 in the process.\n- **Every prohibition** — no force-push, no rebase, no shallow, no wholesale west.yml regeneration, no new `.github/workflows`, no Rust, no `.sh`, no `.mjs`, no Svelte/React authoring, no Kubo-first IPFS, no D1 as a premise on decentralised paths.\n- **Every \"before you conclude X\" rule**, because an agent that does not know it should search will not invoke a skill about searching. The two sections that said this (`「無い」と結論する前に検索する` and `「無い」と言う前に索引を引く`) were merged into one, since they were the same rule stated twice.\n- **The repo-wide resource governor**, which had been buried inside the UI-quality section although it governs every build in the workspace. It is now its own section.\n\n## Result\n\n154,842 -> 92,990 characters (-40%). AGENTS.md regenerates clean (`nbb scripts/gen-agents-md.cljs --check`, exit 0); the generator's substitution anchors were preserved deliberately, since it refuses with exit 2 rather than silently substituting differently.\n\nContainment was checked mechanically rather than by reading: every non-blank line of the old file was compared against the union of the new CLAUDE.md and the four skill files. 139 lines existed in neither. All 139 came from the two merged search sections — the only place text was rewritten rather than moved — and were reviewed one at a time; three affordances that had been dropped in the rewrite (the bare `concept-lookup` invocation, the note that `repo-search` also matches README openings, and the `check-crosswalk` command) were restored.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- **New rules go in CLAUDE.md. New procedure and new measurements go in the skill.** If a measured value is written here it will be quoted next week without its date, which is the failure this document already names twice.\n- **A skill that is never invoked is a rule that is never read.** The delegation only works where the trigger is reliable: a deliberate act (writing `.kotoba`, adding a gate, resolving a conflict) or a hook. Rules whose trigger is the *absence* of a thought — \"before concluding it does not exist\" — cannot be delegated, and were not.\n- The header table in CLAUDE.md names each delegation target, so the split is discoverable without grepping for skills.\n- This changes no rule. Anything that reads differently after the split is a defect in the split, not a decision.\n"}]
